usamts-2005-r1-04.body:
Call a way of writing $P = ab$ with $1 < a < b$ a *factorization* of $P$. Patty and Selma do **not** know the bound 20, so they consider all integers.

**Statement 1** (Patty can't tell): $P$ has at least two factorizations. $P$ has exactly one factorization iff $P = pq$ for primes $p < q$, or $P = p^3$ ($a = p$, $b = p^2$).

**Statement 2** (Selma knew it): every split $S = a + b$, $1 < a < b$, has a product with at least two factorizations, i.e. $S$ is **not** a sum of two distinct primes and **not** of the form $p + p^2$. Since $a, b \le 20$, $S \le 39$. Even $S$ from 8 to 38 are sums of two distinct primes ($8 = 3+5$, $10 = 3 + 7$, ..., $38 = 7 + 31$), and $S \le 7$ fails too ($5 = 2 + 3$, $6 = 2 + 4 = 2 + 2^2$, $7 = 2 + 5$). Checking the odd $S$: $S - 2$ prime rules out $9, 13, 15, 19, 21, 25, 31, 33, 39$, and $p + p^2 \in \{6, 12, 30\}$. What remains is
$$S \in \{11, 17, 23, 27, 29, 35, 37\}.$$
(Call such sums *good*. Every even number from 8 up to a few hundred is a sum of two distinct primes, and $p + p^2$ is always even, so a good sum is always an odd number that is not $2 +$ a prime.)

**Statement 3** (Patty now knows): exactly one factorization of $P$ has a sum on Selma's list (a sum is good only if it is odd and not 2 more than a prime).

**Statement 4** (Selma now knows): exactly one split of $S$ has a product satisfying Statement 3.

**$S = 17$ works with $(4, 13)$.** The splits of 17 are $(2,15), (3,14), (4,13), (5,12), (6,11), (7,10), (8,9)$ with products $30, 42, 52, 60, 66, 70, 72$. For each product other than 52 there are two factorizations with good sums, so Patty could not have deduced the pair:
* $30$: $2+15 = 17$ and $5 + 6 = 11$;
* $42$: $3 + 14 = 17$ and $2 + 21 = 23$;
* $60$: $5 + 12 = 17$ and $3 + 20 = 23$;
* $66$: $6 + 11 = 17$ and $2 + 33 = 35$;
* $70$: $7 + 10 = 17$ and $2 + 35 = 37$;
* $72$: $8 + 9 = 17$ and $3 + 24 = 27$.

For $52 = 2\cdot 26 = 4 \cdot 13$: the sum $2 + 26 = 28 = 5 + 23$ is a sum of two distinct primes (so with sum 28 Selma could not have made Statement 2), while $4 + 13 = 17$ is good. So Patty, holding 52, deduces $(4, 13)$, and Selma, holding 17, sees that only the split $(4,13)$ lets Patty deduce the answer. Every statement of the conversation is true for $(a, b) = (4, 13)$.

**No other sum works.** For each other candidate sum there are at least two splits whose product passes Statement 3, so Selma could not finish:
* $11 = 4 + 7 = 2 + 9$: products $28$ and $18$;
* $23 = 4 + 19 = 7 + 16$: products $76$ and $112$;
* $27 = 4 + 23 = 2 + 25$: products $92$ and $50$;
* $29 = 13 + 16 = 2 + 27$: products $208$ and $54$;
* $35 = 4 + 31 = 3 + 32$: products $124$ and $96$;
* $37 = 8 + 29 = 5 + 32$: products $232$ and $160$.

Each of these products has exactly one factorization with a good sum (marked ✓); all other factorizations have an even sum $\ge 16$ (a sum of two distinct primes, e.g. $16 = 3 + 13$, $22 = 3 + 19$, $28 = 5 + 23$, $58 = 5 + 53$, $106 = 3 + 103$, $118 = 5 + 113$) or an odd sum $2 + \text{prime}$:
* $28$: $4\cdot7$ (11 ✓), $2\cdot 14$ (16). $\quad 18$: $2\cdot 9$ (11 ✓), $3\cdot6$ ($9 = 2 + 7$).
* $76$: $4\cdot 19$ (23 ✓), $2\cdot38$ (40). $\quad 112$: $7\cdot16$ (23 ✓), $2\cdot56$, $4\cdot28$, $8\cdot14$ (58, 32, 22).
* $92$: $4\cdot23$ (27 ✓), $2\cdot46$ (48). $\quad 50$: $2\cdot25$ (27 ✓), $5\cdot10$ ($15 = 2 + 13$).
* $208$: $13\cdot16$ (29 ✓), $2\cdot104$, $4\cdot52$, $8\cdot26$ (106, 56, 34). $\quad 54$: $2\cdot27$ (29 ✓), $3\cdot18$ ($21 = 2 + 19$), $6\cdot9$ ($15 = 2 + 13$).
* $124$: $4\cdot31$ (35 ✓), $2\cdot62$ (64). $\quad 96$: $3\cdot32$ (35 ✓), $2\cdot48$, $4\cdot24$, $6\cdot16$, $8\cdot12$ (50, 28, 22, 20).
* $232$: $8\cdot29$ (37 ✓), $2\cdot116$, $4\cdot58$ (118, 62). $\quad 160$: $5\cdot32$ (37 ✓), $2\cdot80$, $4\cdot40$, $8\cdot20$, $10\cdot16$ (82, 44, 28, 26).

So for each of these sums at least two splits pass Statement 3, and Selma could not have known the pair. Hence the sum is 17, and among the splits of 17 only $(4, 13)$ survives Statement 3.

**Answer: $a = 4$, $b = 13$**
